an. Measured on the passing run: `nav.capped` 90, `safety.interrupt` 0, `watchdog.fired` 0, watchdog 2850 ticks / 570 s clean. |
+| R232 | inform | What the unattended canary cost, and the production bug it found | Recorded rather than asked — no decision owed, but three findings are worth keeping. **(1) PRODUCTION: `bring_to_foreground` cannot work unattended.** `SetForegroundWindow` only obeys a process that already owns the foreground or the most recent input — true of every interactive run and NO unattended one, so a bridge-launched child could not take the window at all. Fixed in `pd2bot/window.py`: `force_foreground()` sends one inert ALT to qualify, attaches to the foreground thread's input queue, asks again, detaches in a `finally`, and still VERIFIES. `bring_to_foreground` escalates to it once. This was blocking any unattended run, not just the canary. **(2) The canary's own mana threshold could not fail.** 99 % against a character at 356/356 mana: `pct <= threshold` is false at exactly 100 %, so rounds 2 and 3 silently proved nothing. Now 100, with the reasoning in the code — the same "a test that can pass without doing the thing it tests" shape this repo has paid for repeatedly, this time caught by the run rather than by review. **(3) A stub snapshot crashed round 5** before it reached the check it existed to make, and took four passing rounds' results down with it. The round now uses a real `Perception` snapshot, and every round is wrapped so a crash becomes a FAIL instead of a lost report. | *(informational — no action owed)* |
+| R231 | verify | Run the two safety canaries — T80 (in-process interrupt) and T81 (watchdog) | Both safety layers are code-complete and validated offline (P1–P4 of `docs/plans/2026-08-07-safety-starvation/`, 1128 tests, ruff clean). Nothing has touched the game since the death, and per the operator's R230(b) choice both layers land before the character re-enters a Cellar. **T80** (`drills/t80_safety_interrupt.py`) proves the in-process half against the real client in two rounds: the wall-clock cap with nothing watching, then a real `SafetyMonitor` armed 1 s into a walk, measuring how long the interrupt takes to arrive. **T81** needs no new code — the watchdog CLI is the instrument: `--probe` first (reads vitals, arms nothing, zero risk), then armed on mana in town so the operator can watch the game pause, then the half that is the whole claim of Track B — kill the bot mid-walk and confirm the watchdog still fires. Both run in TOWN on MANA, the live-test path `safety.py` was built with; life chicken is explicitly disabled in T80 and the interrupt is caught by the drill, so the game is not even left. Asked: be at the machine, tabbed in, and run the four commands in order. | **SUPERSEDED then PASSED, unattended.** The operator instead asked for the canaries to run *without supervision*, with the agent taking the client itself from character select. Feasible and done: `drills/safety_canary.py` (new) takes the window, creates its own game, runs five rounds, tears down in a `finally`, and leaves. Run twice through the elevated bridge. **Run 1 found three defects, two of them in the canary and one in production** — see R232. **Run 2: PASS 5/5.** Cap returned in 2.08 s (budget 2.0); `SafetyInterrupt` raised from inside a walk that had already moved 12 subtiles; a separate watchdog process with no bot running paused the game; `GatedInput` refused world input for the latch while `MenuInput` stayed open; a stale heartbeat made the engine refuse to step. Client left at `main_menu`, no latch, no stray process. |
+| R230 | decision | P2 review gate — Track A is done; may the acceptance battery resume? | The in-process half of the safety fix is implemented and validated offline (P1+P2 of `docs/plans/2026-08-07-safety-starvation/`, results recorded in the phase files). The gate's four questions, answered: (1) the wiring is real — `build_bot` builds the monitor first and hands `safety_poll_for(monitor, should_stop)` to `live_navigator`, pinned by a test that the navigator holds that exact callable; (2) latency MEASURED, not derived — worst case **0.100 s** from crossing to `ChickenExit` across 40 phase offsets with the real monitor and real rate limiter, against **21.8 s** for the same scenario pre-fix (the real death: 24 s); conservatively ≤0.2 s live; (3) the next run proves it via two new log kinds, `safety.interrupt` and `nav.capped`; (4) the residual is the leave itself — ESC → Save-and-Exit still takes its own time, and that is exactly the window the watchdog (P3/P4) closes. Two deviations reported: the give-up ladder now counts across calls (or a capped walk would retry a hopeless target for ever — traverse relies on `NavigationError`, and traverse is what died), and the budget also bounds `_click` (a blocking panel could otherwise hold the tick loop for 10 s, which is what an operator's own ESC creates). Asked: approve resuming the M6 P5 acceptance battery on Track A alone, or wait for the watchdog. | **(b) CHOSEN** (2026-08-07 05:1x) — build the watchdog first; both layers live before the character goes back into a Cellar. The agent's recommendation was (a); the operator's read of the same facts is that the death happened on a supervised run too. Battery stays halted until P3–P5 land. |
+| R229 | decision | Approve the safety-starvation phase table + five design questions | Discovery for the R228(b) fix is done (`docs/plans/2026-08-07-safety-starvation/notes.md`). It found the thing that shapes Track A: `ChickenExit`/`DeathHalt` are `RuntimeError`s, and the path out of `walk_to` runs through at least four broad `except Exception` handlers (`navigate._shake_loose`, the click audit, `town.py:672`, `town.py:2381`) — each correct about its own concern, every one of them able to **swallow a chicken** the moment safety is raised from inside a walk. Proposed a 5-phase `md` plan (P1 interruptible+capped `walk_to`; P2 wiring + telemetry, the gate that unblocks live running; P3 the watchdog process; P4 latch/dead-man/narration/launcher; P5 live canary + ADR + closeout) and five questions: Q1 `SafetyInterrupt(BaseException)` converted at the engine boundary vs re-basing `ChickenExit`; Q2 the 2 s wall-clock cap on the whole `walk_to` call; Q3 watchdog poll 0.2 s with a threshold 5 points below the bot's so the graceful chicken normally wins; Q4 dead-man heartbeat required for unattended runs; Q5 the watchdog as its own process/CLI started by the launcher. | **ALL YES** (2026-08-07 04:2x) — *"yes all. Execute p1&p2."* Phase table approved as proposed and P1+P2 (the offline half, Track A) authorized for immediate execution. Plan written to `docs/plans/2026-08-07-safety-starvation/`. |
+| R228 | decision | Choose the chicken-starvation fix: interruptible `walk_to`, the watchdog process, or both | The T71 run-5 death (chicken starved by a 24 s blocking `walk_to`, `docs/reviews/2026-08-07-chicken-starvation-death/`) owes a safety-critical fix decision; the operator asked for a serious assessment of their idea — a separate chicken process polling HP at ~0.2 s and pressing ESC independently of the bot loop. Assessed (`watchdog-assessment.md` in the review dir): feasible on every axis (multi-process pymem reads are routine — the bridge proves it; CPU is <0.1 % of a core at 5 Hz; separate-process independence beats priority tricks), and the safe design is ESC-pause-only + latch file + dead-man heartbeat, never menu clicks — `UI_ESCMENU_MAIN` is a blocking panel so the bot's own `GatedInput` disarms at send time the moment the menu opens. But it is not a substitute for the in-process fix: the same block also starved the operator's abort channel (`_should_stop`, polled per tick), and the engine's capped-legs contract is what broke. Recommended: in-process safety poll inside `walk_to` + wall-clock cap first (small, offline-testable, regression-pinned), the watchdog as defense-in-depth before the unattended battery. Options put: (a) in-process only, (b) in-process now + watchdog before the battery [recommended], (c) watchdog only [recommended against]. | **(b) CHOSEN** (2026-08-07 04:0x) — the in-process fix lands first (safety poll inside `walk_to` + a wall-clock cap, offline-testable, pinned by a starvation regression test), and the ESC-pause watchdog process is built as an independent second layer before the unattended battery. Planned in `docs/plans/2026-08-07-safety-starvation/`. |
