 | R226 | execute | Drop ten different PD2 maps for T77 to read | The operator asked for the item-exception work to cover PD2 maps, and offered: *"prep a small test - I'll drop 10 different PD2 maps, and you can read the log of the dropped item."* Maps appear under no code in `config/item_codes.toml`, so their kind must be READ — R144 forbids a numeric guess, which is how six wrong elite armours were approved and a Wire Fleece was picked up as a Kraken Shell. `drills/t77_map_identity.py` is written, unit-tested and READ-ONLY (sends nothing, the character does not move). It diffs the floor before and after the drop. The question it exists to answer is not just "what kind is a map" but **one kind or many**, which decides whether the registry needs one entry or a set. Asked: stand somewhere safe, drop ten DIFFERENT maps, type GO. | **PASS** — ten maps read, and the answer is **ten DISTINCT kinds**, not one: 737/740/741/743/746/747/748/750/775 (codes t11, t21, t22, t24, t32, t33, t34, t36, t26) plus **810, which has no code at all** — `item_codes.toml` holds 800 entries, so T42's sweep ended short of it. The codes are a FAMILY, verified afterwards: the table holds exactly 30 `t\d\d` codes, kinds 737-788, with no non-map collision. So the registry should MATCH THE PATTERN rather than list kinds — that covers all 30 known maps including the 21 never sampled, and survives a season renumbering, which a kind list does not. Kind 810 stays an explicit observed member until T42 is re-run. Write-up: `t77-map-identity.md`. |
 | R225 | decision | Re-run T76 after the drill's own defects were fixed | T76 run 1 FAILED INCOMPLETE and could not explain itself: `except InputRefused` printed nothing, so a solo barbarian helm reported "never came up" with zero per-offset lines and no way to tell eight refusals from eight misses — the silent-decision-path shape this project has paid for four times. Also: the census radius was 15 subtiles (borrowed from T63, where the operator drops at their feet) so "saw 1"/"saw 3" could not be distinguished from "outside the radius"; and demanding exact drop counts made the operator responsible for a number the census had to agree with, skipping both crowded rounds. All three fixed — every path prints, radius 40 with the full floor census printed, and crowding is now MEASURED per target (two loose rounds: spread, then packed) instead of asked for. Asked: re-run when convenient. | **PASS (run 2)** — 15 targets across solo/pair/pile, two classes measured both ways, and every path prints now. Headline: in a dense pile **7 of 8 targets could not be picked at all**, each spending the full 8-offset schedule; **6 clicks landed on a neighbour** (T71's inference, now measured directly); and the first offset won only **1 of 15** targets, winners scattered across four different offsets. The hypothesis is therefore only half right — it is not that the winning offset MOVES predictably, it is that for a crowded item often NO offset in the schedule works. This selects Direction C (unstack the pile behaviourally) over Direction B (derive the offset). The run also caught a defect in the drill itself: `classify()` ignored PD2's `s` family (`r05s`, `gzvs`) that R126 had already flagged as the one that actually drops, filing two runes and a gem as "other" — fixed, and both runes had in fact FAILED, one of them at crowd 1. Write-up: `t76-calibration.md`. |
